    "title": "George Magazine to Cease Publication (Published 2001)",
    "text": "Advertisement SKIP ADVERTISEMENT You have a preview view of this article while we are checking your access. When we have confirmed access, the full article content will load. Supported by SKIP ADVERTISEMENT George Magazine to Cease Publication Share full article By Alex Kuczynski Jan. 4, 2001 George, the magazine founded by John F. Kennedy Jr. that combined politics and celebrity in an often unwieldy pairing, will cease publication with the March issue, Jack W. Kliger, the chief executive of Hachette Filipacchi Magazines, announced today. Mr. Kliger said that even though the magazine's circulation had grown in the last year, there was no longer enough advertising support to sustain George. \"With the advertising market softening appreciably in the last two months, and with sinking advertising in the magazine itself, we felt we had no other choice,\" Mr. Kliger said. Frank Lalli,, the magazine's editor in chief since December 1999, said that he was surprised by the announcement, and was told by Mr. Kliger of the decision only this afternoon — as was the magazine's publisher, Daniel M. Lagani — after the news was first reported on the media industry website Inside.com. Mr. Lalli said that Mr. Kliger had had not discussed with him the possibility of closing the magazine, and that Hachette management appeared confident enough in the magazine's prospects that it had recently raised the magazine's cover price. \"In terms of circulation, we were one of the fastest growing magazines in the business,\" Mr. Lalli said. \"But anybody who picked up the magazine could see that we did not have a lot of advertising pages.\" We are having trouble retrieving the article content. Please enable JavaScript in your browser settings. Thank you for your patience while we verify access. If you are in Reader mode please exit and log into your Times account, or subscribe for all of The Times. Thank you for your patience while we verify access. Already a subscriber? Log in . Want